Random Forest is an ensemble learning algorithm used for both classification and regression tasks. It builds a "forest" of decision trees during training and combines their outputs to make more accurate and stable predictions.

It is a collection of multiple random decision trees and it is much less sensitive to the training data.
Randomly select rows from the original data to create the datasets.
Each dataset will contain the same number of rows as the original, though rows might be repeated.
A decision tree is created from every bootstrapped datasets.

Forest: It's a collection of decision trees.
Random: It introduces randomness at two key stages:
Bootstrapping (Bagging): Each tree is trained on a random sample of the data with replacement.
Feature selection: At each split in a tree, only a random subset of features is considered, not all.

To train the tree a subset of the features will be used, not all of them.

